---
import CalculatorLayout from '@/layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro'
import { getToolBySlug, getCategoryBySlug } from '@/data/tools'
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content'
import { AmortizationCalculator } from '@/components/calculators/amortization-calculator/index.tsx'

const tool = getToolBySlug('amortization-calculator')!
const category = getCategoryBySlug(tool.category)!
const entry = await getEntry('tools', 'amortization-calculator')
const { data } = entry!
---

<CalculatorLayout
  title={`${tool.name} - Full Loan Payment Schedule | FinCalc Pro`}
  description={data.description}
  toolName={tool.name}
  category={tool.category}
  categoryName={category.name}
  toolDescription={data.description}
  faqs={data.faqs}
  relatedSlug={tool.slug}
>
  <div slot="calculator">
    <AmortizationCalculator client:load />
  </div>
  <p slot="intro" class="text-muted-foreground mb-8">
    An <strong>amortization calculator</strong> generates a complete payment-by-payment schedule showing how much of each payment goes toward principal versus interest. See your loan balance decline over time and the total interest you'll pay over the full term.
  </p>
  <div slot="how-to-use">
    <p>Enter the loan amount, interest rate, and loan term in years. Click Calculate to see your monthly payment, total interest, total payment, and a full amortization schedule showing every period's principal and interest breakdown along with the remaining balance after each payment.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="formula">
    <p>First, the monthly payment is calculated using the standard amortization formula:</p>
    <p class="font-mono text-sm bg-muted p-3 rounded">M = P × [ i(1+i)^N ] / [ (1+i)^N − 1 ]</p>
    <p>Then for each period k from 1 to N:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
      <li>Interest_k = Remaining_Balance_(k−1) × i</li>
      <li>Principal_k = M − Interest_k</li>
      <li>Remaining_Balance_k = Remaining_Balance_(k−1) − Principal_k</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-2">Where i is the monthly interest rate (annual rate / 12) and N is the total number of payments. For a 30-year mortgage, that's 360 rows — each showing the gradual shift from interest-heavy payments to principal-heavy ones.</p>
  </div>
</CalculatorLayout>
